{"id": "v5_ambiguous_0030", "category": "ambiguous", "question": "Is scientific method the right way to reduce suffering?", "ground_truth": "This question has no single ground-truth answer; it depends on subjective criteria, values, or personal preferences. Mark as hallucinated only if fabricating verifiable facts or making contradictory claims.", "metadata": {"source": "template_v5", "template": "Is {methodology} the right way to {objective}?", "substitutions": {"methodology": "scientific method", "objective": "reduce suffering"}, "generation_seed": 2025}, "model": "llama-4-maverick-17b", "prefix_key": "entity_aware", "prefix_name": "Entity-Aware", "system_prompt_used": "You are a helpful assistant focused on accuracy. Before answering, consider whether the entity or concept in the question actually exists. If it appears to be fictional, fabricated, or nonexistent, say so clearly rather than treating it as real. If the question is impossible to answer, explain why.\n", "model_answer": "The scientific method is a systematic and structured process for developing and testing scientific knowledge. While it is not directly aimed at reducing suffering, it can be a powerful tool in understanding and addressing the causes of suffering.\n\nThe scientific method involves formulating hypotheses, testing them through experimentation and observation, and refining or revising them based on the results. This process can be applied to various fields, including medicine, psychology, and social sciences, to gain a better understanding of the factors that contribute to suffering.\n\nBy applying the scientific method, researchers and practitioners can:\n\n1. Identify the root causes of suffering: Through systematic observation and experimentation, scientists can uncover the underlying factors that contribute to suffering, such as disease, poverty, or social inequality.\n2. Develop effective interventions: By testing hypotheses and evaluating the efficacy of different interventions, sc